shape theory.chemistry.bonding : theory.chemistry.periodic-table, theory.coherence.law1, theory.emergence.composition {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Chemical Bonding from Coherence.
//
// Atoms bond because the composite is more coherent than the
// parts separately. Bonding is emergent composition (Theorem 6.2):
// two atoms in bilateral contact produce an emergent shape with
// lower total energy (more coherent structure).
//
// Three bonding types, each a different contact geometry:
//
// Ionic bonding (electron transfer):
//   Atom A has low ionization energy (few valence electrons,
//   weakly held). Atom B has high electron affinity (nearly full
//   valence shell). A transfers electron(s) to B. Both achieve
//   closed-shell configuration (maximally symmetric, most coherent).
//   The bond is U(1) attraction between the resulting ions.
//   Law 1: the transferred electron closes B's shell; A's remaining
//   electrons close A's shell. Both references are now stable.
//
// Covalent bonding (electron sharing):
//   Two atoms share valence electrons. The shared electrons exist
//   in molecular orbitals spanning both nuclei. This is a new
//   emergent standing wave pattern that doesn't exist for either
//   atom alone.
//   Law 1: shared electrons reference both nuclei simultaneously.
//   The contact shape (the bond) is itself a shape that persists.
//   Single bond: 1 shared pair (sigma). Double: 2 pairs (sigma + pi).
//   Triple: 3 pairs (sigma + 2 pi).
//
// Metallic bonding (electron delocalization):
//   Many atoms share valence electrons across an extended lattice.
//   Electrons form a "sea" of delocalized standing waves.
//   The metallic lattice is an emergent shape whose structure
//   includes all the delocalized electrons.
//   Law 1: each electron references many nuclei. Closure through
//   the extended lattice.
//
// Bond strength = structural weight of the bonding configuration.
// Stronger bonds = more coherent composites = harder to dissolve.
//
// The octet rule: atoms tend toward 8 valence electrons (filled
// s + p subshells). This is the most symmetric configuration for
// n >= 2: all three generator projections (p_x, p_y, p_z) plus
// the spherical s orbital are filled. Maximum symmetry = maximum
// coherence = most stable.
//
// Derives from: theory.chemistry.periodic-table,
//               theory.coherence.law1 (reference closure),
//               theory.emergence.composition (emergent shapes)
  """
}
